ion 0)\n- Opponent's action: Went all-in (-2)\n- Valid actions: All-in or Fold\n\n**Hand Strength:**\nA9 offsuit is a medium-strength hand pre-flop. It's not premium (like AA, KK, QQ, AK), but it's playable. The Ace gives me high card potential, but the 9 kicker is mediocre.\n\n**Opponent's All-in Analysis:**\nMy opponent went all-in pre-flop immediately. This is an aggressive move that could mean:\n1. They have a premium hand (AA, KK, QQ, AK, AQ)\n2. They're bluffing/semi-bluffing with a weaker hand\n3. They have a medium pair (JJ, TT, 99, 88)\n\n**Pot Odds:**\nSince I already called the big blind (100 chips), and opponent went all-in with 20,000:\n- To call, I need to put in 19,900 more chips\n- Pot after call would be ~40,000 chips\n- I'm risking 19,900 to win ~20,100\n- Need roughly 50% equity to break even\n\n**Equity Consideration:**\nA9o against a random hand has about 65% equity. However, against an all-in range (which is typically tight), A9o performs worse:\n- vs. premium pairs (AA-JJ): ~25-30% equity\n- vs. AK/AQ: ~25-30% equity  \n- vs. medium pairs (TT-77): ~45-50% equity\n- vs. weaker aces (AJ-AT): ~25% equity\n- vs. complete bluffs: ~60%+ equity\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- I'm ahead in cumulative chips (+1,750)\n- This is hand 12 of 20, so there's still time to recover\n- Risking my entire stack on A9o is very risky\n- If I fold, I lose 100 chips but preserve my stack\n\nReason:\nWhile A9o has some strength, calling an all-in pre-flop with this hand is too risky. My opponent's all-in likely represents a strong hand, and A9 offsuit is typically behind premium hands. I'm already ahead in the match (+1,750 cumulative), so there's no need to gamble my entire stack on a marginal hand with 8 hands remaining. Folding preserves my chips and allows me to wait for better spots.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
